<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick I of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1657">1657</a>–<a href="/years/1713">1713</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Frederick III, Elector of Brandenburg, Frederick III of Brandenburg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> succeeded as Elector Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1688. In 1701, with the emperor's consent, he crowned himself King in Prussia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, raising the status of the Hohenzollern lands. A patron of the arts and learning, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Prussian Academy of Sciences</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick I of Prussia" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Wilhelm Weidemann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_I_of_Prussia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> on 11 July 1657, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, and Louise Henriette of Orange-Nassau. His cousin on his mother's side was King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of England</a>. On the death of his father on 29 April 1688 he became Elector Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, two lands ruled in personal union as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. Soon after succeeding he founded a new city next to Dorotheenstadt and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrichstadt">Friedrichstadt</a> after himself. Unlike his father, who had sought an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, took Brandenburg into the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> and in 1689 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, yet he styled his court on that of Louis XIV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick wanted the more prestigious title of king, but under Germanic law no kingdom could exist within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Frederick argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had never been part of the Empire and that he ruled it with full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Emperor Leopold I agreed in the Crown Treaty of 16 November 1700, ostensibly in exchange for an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> and 8,000 Prussian troops. Frederick crowned himself on 18 January 1701 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> called this illegal. Because Hohenzollern sovereignty over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> depended on the male line continuing, Frederick took the style King in Prussia rather than King of Prussia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> recognised him first, and he provided it with troops, though he often withheld them until he got the ceremonial concessions he wanted. His royal title applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> only, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> he remained an elector under the emperor. He founded the Prussian Academy of Arts in 1696 and the Academy of Sciences in 1700. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, who first styled himself King of Prussia in 1772, called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> king, because he profited from hiring out Prussian troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick married three times: Elizabeth Henrietta of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> in 1679, Sophia Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, with whom he had the future Frederick William I, and in 1708 Sophia Louise of Mecklenburg-Schwerin. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 25 February 1713 and is entombed in Berlin Cathedral. Frederick the Great wrote of him that he was great in small matters, and small in great matters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick I of Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Longueville line ended in 1707, the estates chose <b>King Frederick I of Prussia</b> as prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-neuchatel">Principality of Neuchâtel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.71,20.51&amp;year=1693" aria-label="Königsberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5001 291 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick I of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5451 561h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.71,20.51&amp;year=1693">What was here in 1693? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
